Users are responsible for complying with the licenses of any models and
binaries they choose to download and run with A-Eye.

Video URL ingestion: When a user supplies a link (e.g. YouTube, Vimeo),
A-Eye uses yt-dlp to download the video to the local machine for analysis.
The user is solely responsible for ensuring such downloads comply with the
source platform's Terms of Service and applicable copyright law. A-Eye does
not circumvent DRM or access controls.

Optional cloud model providers (BYOK): A-Eye runs fully locally by default. A
user may optionally supply their own Claude (Anthropic) or GPT (OpenAI) API key
to route the analysis/chat stages to that provider. When enabled, video frames,
transcripts, and prompts for those stages are transmitted to the chosen provider
and are subject to that provider's terms and pricing. A-Eye stores such keys
locally only and does not transmit them anywhere except to the provider's own
API.
